### Source Evidence
The function `ReadUInt` reads an unsigned integer from an input stream (`IMkvReader`) starting at a specified position (`pos`). The function assumes that the maximum length of the unsigned integer is 8 bytes, which is not always true for all file formats. If the file format allows integers larger than 8 bytes, this assumption could lead to buffer overflow if the reader attempts to read more data than expected.

### Root Cause
The function does not check the size of the data being read against the maximum allowed length of the unsigned integer. It only checks if the read operation was successful and if the remaining space in the buffer is sufficient to store the next byte.

### Repair
To fix this vulnerability, the function should first determine the actual length of the unsigned integer by reading multiple bytes until it encounters a byte with the most significant bit set. This way, the function can correctly handle integers of any size up to the maximum allowed length.

Here's the corrected code:

```c
#include <assert.h>

long long mkvparser::ReadUInt(IMkvReader* pReader, long long pos, long& len) {
  assert(pReader);
  assert(pos >= 0);

  int status;

  len = 1;
  unsigned char b;
  status = pReader->Read(pos, 1, &b);

  if (status < 0)  // error or underflow
    return status;

  if (status > 0) // interpreted as "underflow"
    return E_BUFFER_NOT_FULL;

  if (b == 0) // we can't handle u-int values larger than 8 bytes
    return E_FILE_FORMAT_INVALID;

  unsigned char m = 0x80;

